ype":"Primary documentation"},{"url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/app-pgbasebackup.html","title":"pg_basebackup","passage":"Includes the required WAL (write-ahead log) files in the backup. This will include all write-ahead logs generated during the backup. Unless the method none is specified, it is possible to start a postmaster in the target directory without the need to consult the WAL archive, thus making the output a completely standalone backup.","locator":"--wal-method","origin":"PostgreSQL Global Development Group documentation","sourceType":"Primary documentation"},{"url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/continuous-archiving.html","title":"25.3. Continuous Archiving and Point-in-Time Recovery (PITR)","passage":"To recover successfully using continuous archiving (also called “online backup” by many database vendors), you need a continuous sequence of archived WAL files that extends back at least as far as the start time of your backup. So to get started, you should set up and test your procedure for archiving WAL files before you take your first base backup.","locator":"25.3 introduction","origin":"PostgreSQL Global Development Group documentation","sourceType":"Primary documentation"}],"method":"Current PostgreSQL documentation URL retrieved. Applies to complete-cluster pg_basebackup and continuous-archive recovery, subject to the selected WAL method and recovery configuration.","comparability":"SQLite evidence concerns its backup API's source-lock intervals. PostgreSQL evidence concerns physical cluster backup plus WAL retention/recovery; neither source supplies a comparable workload result.","confidence":"High for documented pg_basebackup and WAL prerequisites because exact primary passages were retrieved.","disposition":"retain"},"eligibleForReview":true,"receiptPath":"/home/balauru/.pi-profiles/fabric/tmp/fabric-research-rework-review/live/worker-backups.json","checked":false},{"key":"backups/r1","assignment":"backups","row":{"id":"r1","slot":"operational-risk","finding":"SQLite warns that an operating-system or power failure while copying a database file can leave the backup database corrupted after recovery.","status":"documented_fact","support":[{"url":"https://www.sqlite.org/backup.html","title":"SQLite Backup API","passage":"If a power failure or operating system failure occurs while copying the database file the backup database may be corrupted following system recovery.","locator":"Online Backup API introduction","origin":"SQLite Documentation","sourceType":"Primary documentation"}],"method":"Applies to copying a database file during a failure. It does not establish the failure behavior of every Backup API implementation or backup destination.","comparability":"No corresponding fault-injection or restore test was retrieved for either system.","confidence":"High for the narrowly worded warning; it is direct SQLite documentation.","disposition":"retain"},"eligibleForReview":true,"receiptPath":"/home/balauru/.pi-profiles/fabric/tmp/fabric-research-rework-review/live/worker-backups.json","checked":false},{"key":"backups/r2","assignment":"backups","row":{"id":"r2","slot":"operational-risk","finding":"For PostgreSQL pg_basebackup with WAL method fetch, required WAL must remain available until collection finishes; recycled WAL makes the backup fail and unusable. Stream instead requires two replication connections and a client that keeps pace with WAL.","status":"documented_fact","support":[{"url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/app-pgbasebackup.html","title":"pg_basebackup","passage":"The write-ahead log files are collected at the end of the backup. Therefore, it is necessary for the source server's wal_keep_size parameter to be set high enough that the required log data is not removed before the end of the backup. If the required log data has been recycled before it's time to transfer it, the backup will fail and be unusable.","locator":"--wal-method: fetch","origin":"PostgreSQL Global Development Group documentation","sourceType":"Primary documentation"},{"url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/app-pgbasebackup.html","title":"pg_basebackup","passage":"Stream write-ahead log data while the backup is being taken. This method will open a second connection to the server and start streaming the write-ahead log in parallel while running the backup. Therefore, it will require two replication connections not just one. As long as the client can keep up with the write-ahead log data, using this method requires no extra write-ahead logs to be saved on the source server.","locator":"--wal-method: stream","origin":"PostgreSQL Global Development Group documentation","sourceType":"Primary documentation"}],"method":"Applies specifically to pg_basebackup WAL collection modes. It is a documented precondition/failure mode, not measured operational reliability.","comparability":"SQLite WAL-page content was fetched but no exact WAL passage could be extracted within the allowance; do not infer equivalence between SQLite WAL files and PostgreSQL archived/streamed WAL.","confidence":"High for the stated pg_basebackup mode conditions because they are exact primary passages.","disposition":"retain"},"eligibleForReview":true,"receiptPath":"/home/balauru/.pi-profiles/fabric/tmp/fabric-research-rework-review/live/worker-backups.json","checked":false}]